    "title": "Trader Joe's to pay $7.4 million over receipt dispute: Are you owed money?",
    "text": "A Columbia man said he’s stranded in Dubai after a work trip and is now waiting for a flight home as travelers across the region struggle to leave. CHARLOTTE, N.C. — A South Carolina man said he’s currently stuck in the Middle East after traveling to Dubai for work and is now hoping for a way back home. Wayne Robinson, a worship arts consultant from Columbia, said he arrived in Dubai about two weeks ago for a business trip. He was scheduled to return to the United States earlier this week, but said a growing backlog of travelers trying to leave the region has made it difficult to find a flight. Robinson said life on the ground in Dubai doesn’t necessarily feel like a war zone. He said much of the city is still operating normally, with what he describes as “business as usual” for many residents and workers. While he waits, Robinson said he’s paying out of pocket to stay in a hotel and that prices have skyrocketed as travelers look for alternative routes out of the region. Robinson continues calling the U.S. State Department hotline for assistance, but said he hasn’t received clear answers about when he might be able to return home. “To see that other countries are coming in and getting their residents and people from the U.S., we’re getting the same emails, the same phone numbers, the same dead ends and nothing is really happening,” Robinson said. “It’s a little bit disheartening. But I believe being here is divine providence, so I’m confident that at the right time, when it’s time for me and others to be back in the U.S., we’ll do that.” Robinson said despite the uncertainty, he remains hopeful. “Anybody who has been exposed to war or wartime, there is some apprehension,” he said. “There’s a plethora of emotions I’m sure everybody goes through because there’s a war that is happening. But my faith is strong. I feel very confident I’ll be able to get out.”dea Robinson mentioned he’s now connecting with other Americans in the area and helping keep spirits up during what he calls a stressful and uncertain time. He’s hopeful a flight scheduled for Wednesday will get him home, but with so many people trying to leave and airspace in parts of the region still closed, he said it’s unclear whether that flight will actually happen. Contact Autumn Bracey at abracey@wcnc.com and follow her on Facebook and Instagram.",
